  volume           = {4},
  abstract         = {The term ‘caesura’ in this paper refers to a point that separates one metrical segment (colon) from another within a periodic unit (line) of verse. The Sanskrit term for what in English I call a caesura is yati, ‘restraint’. Theoretically, the caesura is expected to fall at a boundary between two words, allowing the reciter of a poem to realise it as an audible (śravya) pause (virāma); another common term for the yati is viccheda, ‘split’.¹ These synonymous terms are employed in Sanskrit poetics not only for in-line caesurae, but also for the ends of verse quarters (pādas), which thus},
