  title            = {An Early {Tibetan} History of Indian Medicine},
  annotate         = {A manuscript microfilm from the collection in Rome of Giuseppe Tucci (Rossi-Filibeck 2003: no. 1281). 2 texts, first 46 ff., second 8ff. The second is an outline of the first. two drawings, one of Vāgbhaṭa, and the other of the commentator Candranandana. The author of the work is Che rje. The work formed the basis for part of the history of medicine written by Sangs rgyas rgya mtsho. The latter author gave us a brief notice of the life and writings of Che rje. The Ah. was translated into Tibetan by Lo chen Rin chen bzang po and the Kashmiri Janārdana in AD 1015 (p.311). The tr. was done at Tho ling (Tholing monastery). The present historical work was composed in AD 1204. The main point Martin ends with is that the rGyud bzhi, 4 tantras, is heavily indebted to the Ah., perhaps more than even the studies of Emmerick 1977 etc. show. Che rje's work divides the Ah into four tantras, but doesn't know the rGyud bzhi. There are more MSS of Che rje's work around and it deserves to be edited and published},
