  isbn             = {0195124308},
  keywords         = {historiography},
  annotation       = {Description, Indologist Ronald Inden has in the past raised questions about the images of a `traditional' or `medieval' India employed by colonial scholars and rulers--`Orientalists'--and as also argued that a history of `early medieval' India very different from both the colonial and nationalist accounts could be written. This volume is designed as an important first step towards that goal. The authors loko closely at three genres of texts that have been crucial to the representations of precolonial India. All three essays challenge not only colonialist scholarship but the attempts by religious nationalists to identify Hinduism as the essence of national identity in India and Buddhism as the essence of nationality in Sri Lanka.\\ Review by Richard H. Davis in Journal of Asian Studies (JSTOR), says, p.1410: ``The volume grows out of an irregular colloquium at the University of Chicago concerned with ``Texts and Knowledge" \ldots Inden lays out an overview of their approach to texts in his introduction, drawing on theorists such as V. N. Volosi{11}, Ernesto Laclau, and R. G. Collingwood,\ldots "},
