  note             = {3rd printing, 2001},
  subtitle         = {A Global Theory of Intellectual Change},
  annotation       = {p.524: The social change in the intellectual world consists in focusing attention on a rapidly moving research front; and this in turn comes from technologizing the research front. p.532: The scientific revolution was not the emergence of science. Observational and calculational knowledge existed in all of the major world regions before Europe of the 1600s; p.533:\ldots late [mathematical] texts such as Boethius' ommitted the proofs that constituted the main Greek achievement in abstract mathematics. p.542: There is another, very different structural change in the bases of intellecutal life: displacement of the Church from control of the central means of intellecutal production. p.551: India.\ldots ``Organizationally, the mathematicians, astronomers, and medical doctors werebased in private familistic lineages and guilds, never part of the sustained argument provided by philosophical networks. Public networks of argument did exist in India; its philosophical lineages reached high levels of abstract development. Only mathematics and science were not carried along with it. p.535: What then are the social conditions which brought about this combination of rapid research front plus consensus on older results? One possibility is empiricism. Many kinds of organized empiricism had developed in Europe by the 1600s. [Vesalius 1543, Tycho Brahe 1576--1600]. But the difference between Eruopean rapid-discovery science and trditional science is not a matter of empiricism. Traditional science is essentially empirical. [China, Albert the Great\ldots ] What else is needed? Two other possibilites are \emph{research technology} and \emph{mathematics}.},
