  annotation       = {Three criticisms. 1. AS takes the stories in the Haracaritacintamani at face value, but that's naive. That the HCC asserts that Vajrayana was created by Saivas to destroy them is just self-serving propaganda, not historical data.  2. AS dismisses the autochtonic substratum idea because those who propound it don't give any evidence for it.  Fair enough, argumentum ex silentio.  However, Saivism came from *somewhere* and so did Vajrayana.  So there' remain a lot of open questions about the early history of these religions.  That they share features may indeed point to shared origins, and rather than dismissing that idea, one shuld invesitgate furuther, for example in non-Sanskritic and tribal sources perhaps.  Whatever: the point is, AS's logic is not correct on this point. Finally, 3., AS throughout implies that a Saiva origin for Vajrayana somehow equals inauthenticity.  At the end of his paper he does try to rectify this by saying that the Buddhists had their own rationale etc.  But the fact that one religion borrows from another does not actually say anything about authenticity, but just about history.  Christianity is an offshoot of Judaism and Gnosticism.  But it has become it's own thing and its historical origins are not considered to be a defining factor in understanding how it functions and what it offers aspirants},
  file             = {:Sanderson, Alexis/1995 - Vajrayāna_ Origin and Function.pdf:PDF;:/home/dom/Dropbox/Digital Library extracts/Assorted/Buddhism/will-2005 - Buddhism_ Critical Concepts in Religious.pdf:PDF},
  modificationdate = {2019-01-12T00:00:00},
  owner            = {dom},
}

