  citationkey      = {aiya-sour},
  date             = {1986},
  location         = {Delhi},
  publisher        = {Gian Publishing House},
  title            = {Sources of Vijayanagar History},
  note             = {Reprint},
  annotation       = {Section \#90, pp.269--84, is a summary of the \emph{Sāhityaratnākara} of Yajñanārāyaṇa Dīkṣita, son of Govinda D. ``This Sanskrit poem deals with the life and achievements of the famous Tanjore Nāyaka ruler Raghunātha. It was written by YD, son of the famous GD, minister of the Tanjore Nāyaka rulers Achyta and Raghunātha." In sarga 1, GD is said to be a great authority in Advaita Vedanta and in the six darśanas (p.274): \emph{advaitavidyāvibhavāvalambān govindayajvendragurūn upāse /\\ nirmathya ṣaḍdarśananīradhīnye dhiyaiva sārān dadate budhebhyaḥ} \\ p.270: at end of sarga III, Raghunātha is said to be supporting 1000 Brahman families at Champakāṭavi (Mannarguḍi). After R. was born and educated in sāhitya (arts), he was directed by his father to compose a poem dealing with the life of Krishna, since by literary works alone could one become immortal (p.276:)\emph{rāmo daśasv avatareṣv api rājate 'sāv\\// adyāpi kīrtivapur ādikaver nibandhāt// tasmād vidhehi samatām adadhehi tāvad // yatnaṃ vibhor yad upateś caritaprabandham//27//}. \\ About a dozen works in Sanskrit and Telugu ascribed to R. have survived. His Telugu poem Rāmāyanam ``is highly praised for its merits, and it has been translated into Sanskrit by Madhuravāṇi , another poetess that adorned his court. R. helped the king of Jaffna (Yāḷpāṇa -> N\=epāḷa) to protect himself against the Portuguese (pārasīkas). \\ p.271: Sarga IX when autumn arrived, King Acyuta remembered the promise he had made to the king of Jaffna and held a council with his minister Govinda Dīkṣita and his son Raghunātha in the palace Lakshmīvilāsam. \\ Sarga X: A, R, and GD hold council. GD described theaggressions of the Portuguese upon the Jaffna king. He then proceeded to give an account of Cholaka (Śoḍ{l}aga) their allay. a cruel torturer and murderer who had occupied an island near the coast and was helping the Portuguese. (p.272) GD then sayd that the king of the Pāṇdya (Nāyaka of Madura) was hostile to him, and had allied with Śoḷaga and the Nāyaka of Gingī, Krishnappa. (account of Krishnappa in Purschas his pilgrimes, vol. X, chapter VII, jesuit observations of India.)},
  modificationdate = {2018-09-24T00:00:00},
  owner            = {dom},
  source           = {Cambr. Oriental Inst. Libr. IB W 24},
}

