  title            = {On Editing {Indian} Codices Unici (with Special Reference to the {Gilgit} Manuscripts)},
  annotation       = {The discovery of extra Gilgit folia enables one to check the Tibetan-to-Sanskrit renderings in the editions of Dutt and Gnoli. In all cases the recreated Skt. is not the same as that given in the newly-found MS leaves. p.63: ``\ldots retranslations [from Tibetan into Sanskrit], however brilliant they may be, are approximations at best, lacking all philological value." p.59, cites Maas, Textual Criticism (Oxford 1958), p.1 ff.: The business of textual criticism is to produce a text as close as possible to the original (\emph{constitutio textus}) \ldots The tradition rests either on a single witness (\emph{codex unicus}) or on several. In the former case, \emph{recensio} consists in describing and deciphering as accurately as possible the single witness; in the latter it is often a very complicated business. p.59--60 cites Katre, Intro. to Ind. Text Crit., Poona 1954, p.37 on editing from the \emph{codex unicus}},
