@Article{manz-sirr,
  author           = {M. A. Manzalaoui},
  citationkey      = {manz-sirr},
  date             = {1970/1971},
  journaltitle     = {Oriens},
  pages            = {147--257},
  title            = {The Pseudo-{Aristotelian} \emph{Kitāb, Sirr, al-Asrār}: Facts and Problems},
  volume           = {23--24},
  keywords         = {alchemy, emerald tablet, hermes trismegistus},
  annotation       = {p.162: ``Paradoxically, if the portions of the \emph{Sirr} that discuss statecraft and ethics remind us more closely of the Persianized hellenic tradition, those passages which attach occult significance to the physical world --- the herbal, the lapidary, and the astrological and alchemical sections --- remind us closely of the hellenised Persian tradition: \ldots". \\ p.164: ``The question of the origin of the \emph{Sirr} remains entirely open." \\p.200, concerning the lists of personal qualities appropriate to fighting men and religious men: ``That one should not too closely particularize the source of passages bearing a general resemblance, is brought home to one by the striking similarity to our lists, of two catalogues of qualities, found in the \emph{Arthasastra}. This Indian Mirror for Princes does indeed contain some notable resemblances to the \emph{Sirr}, and the question of its possible influence upon it is discussed below." \\ p.203: ``The \emph{Sirr} is not connectred with the shorter alchemical texts atttibuted to Aristotle, nor with the other alchemical texts that bear the same title of \emph{Sirr al-asrār} or \emph{Secretum} (var.lec. \emph{Secreta}) \emph{secretorum} (see, e.g., Ferguson's \emph{Bibliotheca chemica [74]}). The best-known of these homonymous works is by al-Rāzī [203], and has been edited and translated both by Ruska and by Karimov (see below, p.243)." \\ p.210: ``The passages which refer to Indians and Turks offer no certain indications of late compostition, but, on the contrary, are paralleled by similar passages in earlier literature. There are several references to [kttb al-Hnd]: this should probably be vocalized \emph{kuttāb al-Hind} (`the writers of India') and not \emph{kitāb al-Hind} (`the Book of India') (there are references to `\emph{kitāb al-Hind}' in the \emph{`Uyūn al-akhbār} of Ibn Qutaybah (828--89 A.D.) -v. 430). Indian culture was known to the Seleucids, in whose days Megasthenes' work on India was written. The description of the `custom of the Indians' in \emph{Sirr} II has an analogue in this work. J. W. M'Crindle, \emph{Ancient India as described by Megasthenes and Arrian} [173], quotes a passage from the book, as known to Strabo (XV.i. 53 ff.), describing the Indian king's hunting: \begin{quote}`Crowds of women surround him, and outside of this circle spearmen are ranged. The road is marked off with ropes, and it is death, for man and woman alike, to pass within the ropes'.\end{quote} For knowledge of Indian in Hellenistic times, we have Philostratus' \emph{Life of Appolonius of Tyana} [192] -- mainly Books II and III (there is further information in Jarl Charpentier, \emph{The Indian Travels of Appolonius of Tyana} and in O'Leary [188], p.126. Hellenistic and Roman sources are dealt with in Dihle's \emph{India in Hellenistic Literature} [58].\par The knowledge of India among the Arabs \ldots. Cureton and Wilson, in their \ldots\ \emph{Extract from `Uyūn al-anbā} [50] survey the books of the Indians which were known to the Arabs." \\pp.210-12 discuss resonances in Indian literature, including the Bhagavadgita and the Vedas; reference to an important but unpublished paper by Dr. Stig Wikander of Uppsala, summarized in print [264], that says there are parallels, though they are never verbal ones, between the contents of Indian Mirrors for Princes and of the \emph{Sirr}. Refs. Arthasastra. p.215: ``The discourse on Warfare (Bad. 151) mentions the merits of soldiers recruited from \emph{al-Hind} and among \emph{al-Turk}. \\p.237, refers to the work of Ruska, Plessner and Kraus on the \emph{Tabula Smaragdina}" \\p.237: ``Kraus places the \texttt{Tabula} as the closing words of the \emph{Sirr al-khalīqah} (Kraus [153] 2/272)\ldots"},
  file             = {:1970 - The Pseudo Aristotelian Kitāb, Sirr, Al Asrār_ Facts and Problems.pdf:PDF},
  modificationdate = {2019-01-12T00:00:00},
  owner            = {dom},
}

