  volume           = {55},
  url              = {http://www.jstor.org/stable/1508725},
  annotation       = {A very interesting piece. Gives a historical, sketch of the Greek Cynics, and then of the Pāśupatas. Refers to forthcoming work on the Pāśupata Sūtras by Minoru Hara, and gives a tr. of ch.2 of the PS, with comm., in which the adherent is advised to seek opprobrium in order to swap his bad karma for the good karma of the onlooker. Ingalls cites parallels in Manu for the idea of (de)merit transference. He suggests that ``Lakulīśa" (Lakuleśa ``staff-lord"?) may be a version of the Greek ``Heracles", the staff-wielding patron of the Cynics. He sees both cults deriving in parallel from shamanistic practices from the Black Sea area.},
