  title            = {A Descriptive Catalogue of {Sanskrit} Manuscripts of {Orissa} in the Collection of the {Orissa State Museum}. Volume I: Smṛti Manuscripts},
  keywords         = {catalogue},
  annotation       = {Introduction gives a useful survery of Orissan Skt authors on dharma etc. and their lineages and networks. p. ii: Śatānanda Ācārya fl. 1099, at Puri (= Puruṣottama), wrote on jyotiṣa and dharma. p. iii: Śrīdhara śarma. From a family proficient in śruti, smṛti and jyotiṣa, from Kotarāvaṅga viṣaya or Kotarāhaṅga pargana of the Puri district, patronized by the Bhanja royal family. p.iv: Nīlāmbara ācārya, father of the poet Udayana ācārya and Govardhana ācārya. Fl. ca. 1180. ibid: Śaṃkhadhara Before 1300. v: Śambhukara Vājapeyī and his son Vidyākara Vājapeyī Descendants still living in the village Daṇḍa Mukuṃndapur near Pipili on the Jagannadha Road. Fl. 1330. Quite a lot about these two. xi. Rāmacandra Vājapeyī. Fl. ca. 1500. xiv. Bṛhaspatisūrī Wrote Kṛtyakaumudī. xv. Kālidāsa chayanī. Fl. before Narasiṃha Vājapeyī (1520--1570). xv. Yogīśvara pātra. Fl. ca. 1413--1434. xv. The family of Godāvara Miśra and Narasiṃha Vājapeyī Mahapatra gives a family tree of the Godāvaras on p. xvi. Mantrivara Rājaguru Godāvara Miśra . Known as a tantra specialist, with effective mantras for flooding the Godāvarī. Fl. ca 1510 He wrote a Yogacintāmaṇi in which he quoted 47 works. xxxi. Gajapati Puruṣottama Deva},
  modificationdate = {2018-09-17T00:00:00},
  owner            = {dom},
}

