  journaltitle     = {Bhāratīya Vidyā},
  pages            = {33--4},
  title            = {{Bhāskara Bhaṭṭa's} Work on Anatomy Called the {Śārīra-Padminī} and Its Wrong Dating by {Dr.\ Hoernle}},
  volume           = {VII},
  note             = {Cited from \citealt[189--92]{gode-silh2}},
  annotation       = {Hoernle put the work at AD 1000. Gode gives evidence from a MS he has seen belonging to Rajavaidya S. A. Jagatap of Kolhapur and dated AD 1791, that Bhāskara completed the work in saṃvat 1735, or AD 1677/8. There's a chronogram in the work itself that says this. Bhaskara was the son of Āpāji (Benares vaidya), son of Haribhaṭṭa, son of Puruṣottamabhaṭṭa of Tryambakeśvarapura. The family was of the Kāśyapagotra and surnamed Agnihotri. These details from Bhāskara's \emph{Śārīrapadminī} and his other works such as the \emph{Smṛtiprakāśa} and the \emph{Padmāmṛtataraṅgiṇī}. According to Hoernle, the \emph{Śārīrapadminī} describes the skeleton according to Suśruta as modified by Vāgbhaṭa.},
