  url              = {https://www.jstor.org/stable/41688391},
  annotation       = {Notes that Appaya opposed Ārya's view on the dogmatic grounds that it opposed śruti, which says ``pṛthivī pratiṣṭhā". ``\emph{svargapṛthivyoḥ parasparam upajīvyopajīvakabhāvasya tulyatayā svargo na pratiṣṭhā pṛthivī pratiṣṭheti vyavasthitoktyayogāt/ kiṃ tu niścalatvam iha pratiṣṭhāśabdārthaḥ/ tat tu jyotiścakrāntargatatvena bhramati svarge nāsti asti tu pṛthivyāṃ pṛtivī pratiṣṭheti śrutyantarāt/ āryabhaṭādyabhimatabhūbhramaṇādivādānāṃ śrutinyāyavirodhena heyatvāt}". This is taken from p.201 of the \emph{Vedāntakalpatāruparimala}, ed. Rāmaśāstri Tailaṅgaṇa (Vizianagaram Sanskrit Series, Benares, 1895) Amalānanda (fl.1250), author of the \emph{Vedāntakalpatāru}, lived in Nāsik \citep[49, note 2]{gode-appa}. \\ Gode refers to Makkibhaṭṭa, commentator on Śrīpati's \emph{Siddhāntaśekhara} as, (p.52): ``the only writer of repute to uphold Āryabhaṭa's view". Gode, p.52, note 1, says ``As contrasted with this dogmatic view of Appaya the following statement of Śaṃkarācārya reflects the correct attitude of a philosopher towards matters within the ken of our senses:-- `na śrutiśatam api śīto'gnir aprakāśo veti bruvan prāmāṇyam upaiti' (Vide p.5 of \emph{Indian Culture and its Claims}, a paper read by Pandit Srikrishna Joshi at the Foundation Ceremony of the Benares Hindu University, 1916\ldots .) Aufrecht records a work called `Bhūbhramavādakhaṇḍananirāsa' by scholars in Sihor (C.C.I, p.415a).},
  file             = {:Gode, PK/1938 - Notes on Indian Chronology No.,XXXVII_ Appayadīkṣita's Criticism of Āryabhaṭa's Theory of the Diurnal Motion of the Earth (bhūbhramavāda).pdf:PDF},
