  title            = {Alpines in Eastern India},
  volume           = {3},
  abstract         = {p. 162: the "Alpines" are a group who entered India in the 3rd millennium BC, coming from the Iranian table-land and spoke IE of the Dardic or Piśachi type. One branch went south-west, and one skull has been discovered at Mohenjodaro.  The other branch moved towards Eastern India without ... staying long in the central part of Northern India. ... They gave rise to Grierson's "outer band" of the IA languages, Bihari, Bengali, Assamese and Oriya.   During the 2nd millennium BCE the Vedic Aryans entered India through Afghanistan. p. 163: Barua rejects Suniti Kumar Chatterjee's theory of two Aryan waves, an early one designated Vrātyas and a later one of Vedic Indians.  He calls this an adaptation of Hoernle's speculation of a second Aryan intrusion through the Himalayas. Barua calls his theory the same as that of Rai Bahadur Ramaprasad Chanda's theory of a tribe of the Homo-Alpinus entering India and pushing towards the east giving rise to the Pisachi languges of Eastern India, etc. (p. 164)},
