@Book{alte-hist,
  author           = {A. S. Altekar},
  citationkey      = {alte-hist},
  date             = {1937},
  location         = {Benares},
  publisher        = {Anant Sadashiv Altekar for The Culture Publication House, Benares Hindu University},
  title            = {History of {Benares} from Pre-historic Times to the Present Day with Six Plates},
  note             = {First published in the \emph{J. Benares Hindu University} in 2 parts.},
  annotation       = {03.7.30, scholars: pp.38--43, 47--49 pp.59--63: Balwant Singh (d.1770) p. 38: no evidence is so far forthcoming to show that any important Sanskrit works were composed at Benares during the period 1200--1500 A.D. This seems to be due to the fact that Sanskrit scholarship suffered considerably during this period by the flight of some scholarly families and the persecution of those [p.38] that remained behind.\footnote{Statements like {\dev saṃprāpte tu kalau kāle vindhyādreruttare sthitāḥ| brāhmaṇā yajñarahitā jyotiḥśāstraparāṅmukhāḥ| vindhyasya dakṣiṇe bhāge yatra godāvarii nadī| tatra vedāś ca yajñaś ca bhaviṣyanti kalau yuge||} that occur in Smṛtis like Vyāsa are obviously inspired by the actual observation of the condition of Sanskrit scholarship in places like Benares during 1200--1500 A.D.} Gone for Benares were the days when royal patronage used to offer a powerful incentive to the cause of learning and scholarship. Bernier, who visited Benares by the middle of the 17th century, has observed that Benares students were no doubt studying for ten or twelve years, but could entertain no hopes whatever that honour and emoluments might be the reward of extraordinary attainments.\footnote{Bernier, \emph{Travels in the Mogul Empire}, p.334.} In spite of this, however, Benares scholars soon adjusted themselves to the new circumstances, and we find the lamp of Sanskrit learning burning luminously at Benares from the 16th century. \footnote{The Deccan itself had passed under the Muslim rule in the 14th century, and so the exodus to the south must have come to an end. During the period 1200--1400 A.D., the lamp of Sanskrit learning was burning at Benares, but burning very dimly.} Benares once more became the foremost place of Hindu religion and Sanskrit learning in spite of its Muslim Government. It once more began to attract scholars from the farthest corners of India, who were held in the highest veneration on their return home at the completion of their education. Several learned families of Maharashtra and Karnatik came and settled down permanently at Benares by the beginning of the 16th century. Dharmādhikāri, Śeṣa, Bhaṭṭa and Mouni were the chief among them. Members of these families and their disciples dominated Benares scholarship for more than three centuries.\footnote{I am indebted to Pandit Narayana Shastri Khiste, Librarian, Manuscript Library, Queen's College, Benares, for some of the information given in this section.} Nanda Pandit, the famous author of \emph{Dattaka-mīmāmsā}, (c. 1570--1630 A.D.) was a member of the Dharmādhikāri family, which had migrated to Benares [p.40] from Bidar by the beginning of the 126th century A.D.\footnote{This family possesses a copy of a \emph{tāmrapaṭṭa}, said to have been issued by King Anaṅgapāla in the 11th century A.D., conferring upon the Deccan Brahmins the right of \emph{paurohitya} over Benares, Prayāga and Gayā. The original of this copy is not available, and its genuineness is doubtful as king Ana\.gapāla is not known to have been ruling over these cities in the 11th century A.D.} \emph{Paraśurāma-prakāśa} is another work on Dharmaśāstra, written by Khaṇḍerāya, another member of this family probably in the same century. The Śeṣa family, which also came to Benares by the beginning of the 16th century,\footnote{For a detailed history of the Śeṣa and Bhaṭṭa families, see \emph{Ind. Ant.} Vol. XLI, pp. 245--253 and pp. 7--15. Both families came to Benares from the bank of the Godāvarī river; the precise places are not known.} has made important contributions to several branches of Sanskrit literature.\footnote{Of these seven were on Sanskrit and Prakrit gram{3}, viz., \emph{Upapadamatiṅ-sūtravyākhyāna, Padacandrikā, Prakriyāprakāśā} [\emph{sic}], \emph{Prākṛtacandrikā, Yaṅlugantaśiromaṇi, Śabdālaṅkāra and Sphoṭatatva}, three on Kāvya, viz., \emph{Kaṃsavadha, Pārijātaharaṇacampū, Murārivijayarmaśāstra}, viz., \emph{Śūdrācāraśiromaṇi}.} His brother Cintāmaṇi also composed several works, \emph{Rasamanjarī-parimala} being a noteworthy one amongst them. Vīreśvara, a son of Kṛṣṇa, has not left behind any works, but he was the preceptor of three distinguished disciples, who have made very important contributions to different branches of Sanskrit literature. The first among them was Jagannātha Paṇḍitarāja,\footnote{This title is still exclusively used to denote Jagannātha by modern Pandits.} the author of \emph{Rasagaṅgādhara, Manoramākucamardanam} and several other Sanskrit works.\footnote{Among his works may be mentioned \emph{Amṛtalaharī, Karuṇālahari, Citramīmānsākhandanaṁ, Pīyūṣalaharī Bhāminīvilāsa, Lakṣmīlaharī, Sudhālaharī}, and \emph{Asafavilāsa}.} The second was Bhaṭṭojī Dīkṣita, the famous author of \emph{Siddhāntakaumudī}, which is [p.41] still studied in every Sanskrit \emph{Pāṭhaśālā}. The third was Annambhaṭṭa, the author of \emph{Tarkasaṅgraha}. All these scholars were prolific writers and flourished in the 17th century. The Bhaṭṭa family, which immigrated to Benares in the 16th century, also made important contribution to Sanskrit literature. Bhaṭṭa Nārāyaṇa, who flourished in the latter half of the 16th century, was the author of several works, \emph{Prayoga-ratna} and \emph{Tristhalīsetu} being the chief ones among them. He re-erected the temple of Viśvanātha in c. 1585 on its old site. He was given the title of \emph{Jagadguru}, `the preceptor of the universe', and he exercised profound influence over later writers. His son \'Saṅkarabhaṭṭa wrote \emph{Dvaitanirṇaya, Vratamayūkha} and \emph{Gādhivaṁśānucaritakāvya}. The last work is a history of the family, its \emph{gotra} being Viśvāmitra. Śaṅkarabhaṭṭa's son was Nīlakaṇṭha. He belonged to the first half of the 17th century and composed \emph{Bhagavantabhāskara} consisting of twelve \emph{Mayūkhas}, each dealing with a different topic of Dharmaśāstra. Nīlakaṇṭhas's cousin, Kamalākarabhaṭṭa, is the author of \emph{Nirṇayasindu}, a book on Dharmaśāstra, most frequently consulted by modern Pandits.\footnote{Kamalākara was a grandson of Nārāyaṇa and a son of Rāmakṛṣṇa.} Raghunātha, another member of this family, has composed \emph{Kālatatvavivecana} and \emph{Gotrapravaranirṇaya}. Viśvesvarabhatṭṭa, alias Gāgābhaṭṭa, who was invited by Sivaji to Raigad on the occasion of his coronation, was also a member of the Bhaṭṭa family. He was an erudite scholar and voluminous writer and his declaration that Sivaji was a full-ledged Kṣatriya showed that he was a man of vision not bound down by traditional views. He has composed a few works on Mīmāmsā and more than a dozen books on Dharmaśāstra.\footnote{Among his works the following may be mentioned here: \emph{Kāyasthadharmadīpa, Prayogasāra, Sapiṇḍavicāra, Mīmāmsākusumāñjalī, Rākāgama} on poetics and thirteen \emph{Nibandhas} on Dharmaśāstra topics known as \emph{uddyotas}. These latter were commenced by his father, Dinakara and completed by Gāgābhaṭṭa.} Among other authors of the 17th century may be mentioned Raṅgojī dīkṣita, a brother of Bhaṭṭoji Dīkṣita, [p.42] who has composed \emph{Advaitacintāmaṇi} and \emph{Advaitaśāstrsāroddhāra}. He is known by the title \emph{Dvaitaśāstraśatru}. His son Koṇḍabhaṭṭa was a famous grammarian, his work \emph{Vaiyākaraṇabhūṣaṇa} being at present prescribed in several Sanskrit colleges. Among the eighteenth century celebrities of Benares may be mentioned Nāgojībhaṭṭa Kāle, the author of several works on Dharmaśāstra, Mīmāmsā and Sāhitya, and his pupil Vaidyanātha Pāyaguṇ\d de, who wrote commentaries on almost all the works of his preceptor. Vaidyanātha's wife Lakṣmiībai is the reputed author of erudite commentaries on the \emph{Mitākṣarī} and the \emph{Kālanirṇaya}.\footnote{Lakṣmī's authorship of these commentaries is extremely doubtful. The introduction and colophon no doubt definitely ascribe the work to Lakṣmī, but the evidence of style and the reference to Nagojībhaṭṭa as the \emph{preceptor} of the author show that Vaidyanātha himself was the author of the work. Nāgojībhaṭṭa was the \emph{teacher} of Vaidyanātha and not of Lakṣmī. } The contributions of Benares to Vedānta during the period was not negligible. In the 16th century Nārasimhāśrama composed \emph{Advaitadīpikā} and \emph{Tatvaviveka}. During the next century two famous works were composed in Benares on Vedānta, \emph{Aṇubhāṣya} and \emp{Advaitasiddhi}. The former was composed at Benares by Vallabhācārya, the founder of the Śuddhādvaita school, who spent the evening of his lie at Benares, where he died at Hanuman ghat. \emph{Advaitasiddhi}, which is one of the most erudite, ingenious and difficult works on Advaita Vedānta, was composed here by Madhusūdana Sarasvatī. The works on Advaita of Raṅgojībhaṭṭa have been mentioned already. In the sphere of Nyāya the contribution of Benares was not considerable. Nadia was a more celebrated centre of Nyāya scholarship than Benares. Bhavadeva Siddhāntavāgīśa\footnote{This scholar has written several commentaries on Nyāya works.} [p.43] and his pupil Mahādevaśāstrī Puntambekar\footnote{\emph{Nyāyakaustubha}, written by this scholar, has been published in the Sarasvatībhavana series.} are the principal Nyāya authors of Benares; they flourished in the 1th century. Contribution of Benares to Sanskrit scholarship and literature during the period 1500--1800 A. D. is undoubtedly much greater than that of any other three contemporary centre of Sanskrit learning put together. Benares had succeeded in shaking off the paralysis that had overtaken her literary activity during the three previous centuries as a result of Muslim rule. She learnt how to adjust herself to the new depressing and discouraging situation. Of course it is true that most of the works produced by Benares were not independent works. They were of the nature of commentaries and compilations with a few exceptions. This observation, however, holds good of about 90\% works written in Sanskrit during the second millennium of the Christian era. The credit for retaining the popular faith in the old culture and religion of the land belongs in no small degree to a number of saint poets and authors of the Bhakti School, who flourished all over India during this dark and dismal age. The contribution of Benares in this respect is remarkable. Rāmānand, one of the early and earnest Vaiṣṇava reformers, flourished in Benares during the 14th century; he used to live near the Panchaganga ghat. During the next century flourished his disciples Kabir and Raidas; of these the former was born in the precincts of Benares and his \emph{samādhi} has given the name to the Kabirchaura \emph{mohalla} of the city. Kabir was soon followed by Tulsidas, the famous author of the \emph{Rāmacharitamānasa}, who carried on his religious and literary activities at Benares till his death in 1623 A. D. Benares may well be proud of the religious and literary work of these three writers, who made the city a centre of the medieval Bhakti movement. Both Guru Nanak and Chaitanya, the leaders of the Bhakti movement in the Punjab and Bengal respectively, visited Benares in order to expound their gospel religion and culture},
  modificationdate = {2022-12-09T15:19:29},
  owner            = {dom},
  source           = {BL shelfmarks: 010057.k.59 and V 1307 (2 copies)},
}

