  volume           = {21},
  annotation       = {p.2 ff ``an independent treatise based for hte most part on the Nyāyasūtras of Gautama \ldots makes use only of those Sūtras which fall within the scheme fo his work and the contents of which do not differ from the views he himself held on the different topics of the work. This is probably responsible for the fact tha twhile upto page 147 the work is wholly based on the Nyāyasūtras, pages 148-170 contain, leaving of course some exceptions, his own definitions for Jātis and Nigrahasthānas. \ldots He has made use, for the most part, only of that portion of the Nyāyaūtras, which deals with the art of discussion, with the exclusion of that which is concerned with the philosophical discussions" \par Introduction, p.9 describes how P. Nārāyaṇa Shastri Khiste, the Assistant Librarian of Saraswati Bhavan Library, Kāśī, has recently purchased a collection of Sanskrit MSS from the descendants of Mahīdhara, the commentator of hte Yajurveda, containing many manuscripts written in his own hand. \parThe author Jayarāma is the pupil of Rāmabhadra Siddhāntavāgīśa (intro. p.17ff), pupil of Jagadīśa (a. of Kāvyaprakāśarahasya). Jayarāma fl. 1657, since he is one of the signatories of the Decision or Vyavasthā of Benares Pandits in saṃ 1714, published by R. S. Pimputkar in the bōk "Citalebhaṭṭapūrkaraṇa" (1926), pp. 78--81. In 1657, Jayarāma was old, so his period of activity was ca. 1600--1657},
