  keywords         = {catalogue},
  annotation       = {Ranbir Singh > son Amar Singh. > son Harisingh Under Amar's rule, "Scholars were invited from all parts of India to take charge of the schools, libraries, and research institutions that were established in Srinagar, Jammu, and other important places. A staff of Pandits and Maulvis was engatged in the translation of important Persian and Arabic classics into the vernacular and the compliation and publication of authoritative works on Hindu law, ritual, astrology, medicine, etc. The extent fo the Maharaja's interest in Sanskrit is revealed by the fact that he had a military manual prepared with the intention of replacing the English terms of command by Sanskrit ones. Even the instructions regarding the bringing up of the princes, his children, and their daily time-table were issued in Sanskrit\ldots . The late Raja Sir Ram Singh's manuscript library consiting of nearly two thousnad volumes has been transferred in toto to the Raghunath Temple Library of Jammu, but is not included in Sir Aurel Stein's catalogue of that library. \par The Sanskrit library of Raja Sir Amar Singh, now in the possession of his son, General Sir Harisingh, \ldots consists of 1325 volumes, of which 274 are manuscripts adn the rest printed books. " -- Introduction, p.1.},
