@Book{sath-hist,
  author           = {R. {Sathyanatha Aiyar}},
  citationkey      = {sath-hist},
  date             = {1924},
  editor           = {S. {Krishnaswami Aiyangar}},
  location         = {Madras},
  publisher        = {Oxford University Press},
  title            = {History of the Nayaks of Madura},
  series           = {The Madras University Historical Series},
  volume           = {II},
  annotation       = {p.124, note 43 (cont.) discusses the aggressive missionary activity of the Portuguese in the 16 century, in S. India. The Nayak of Tanjore, Achyta, had to turn them out of Negapatam by force. ``They transferred their political activity thereafter on the Tanjore coast to D\=evikoṭṭa at the mouth of the Colroon, and were declared by Govinda Dikshita, the minister, to be the power behind the chieftain who harried the country round and assumed with an attitude of defiance against his liege lord, the Nāyak of Tanjore." Broadly, ``the Madura Nāyak was actively in alliance with the Portuguese, and Tanjore and even Ramnad in alliance with the Dutch." \\ p.34, on sources: ``The \emph{Tanjāvūri Āndhra Rājula Charitra},\footnote{S.K.A., \emph{Sources}, p.319, \emph{seq.}} similar, in the main, to the \emph{Tanjāvūri Vāri Charitra} of Taylor [William Taylor, 1835, \emph{Oriental Historical Manuscripts}, not in CUL], is an illuminating record, which bears specially on the history of Tanjore in the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries, but also throws much light on Madura affairs, principally on the foundation of the Nāyakship." \\ pp.166--71: The story of Venkaṇṇa, and the Mahratta conquest and occupation of Tanjore is clearly told. \\pp.333--72: appendix D gives a chronological list of Tamil, Telugu and other inscriptions, with brief summaries, spanning 1475--1797.\\ p.344: inscription \#80. ``1586. Sanskrit in Nāgari; Ś.S. 1508, Vyaya; Madura; the Daḷavāy Agrahāram Plates of Venkaṭapati I. gift of the village of Gangavārappaṭṭi or Vīrabhūpasamudram to a number of Brahmans at the request of Vīrappa Nāyaka, the son of KrishṇappaṄāyaka and grandson of Viśvanātha. (E.I., xii, pp.159--87.)" \\p.346, \#92. `` Sanskrit in Grantha; \ldots " celebrates the grant of the village Naḍikkuḍi or Ativīrarāmapuram to a number of Brahmans. (S.C.P., no.211; T.A.S., i, pp.13--46.). Again in 1597 (2 villages for Vaiṣṇava Brahmans), and 1598 (gift of a village to a number of Brāhmaṇas), and 1598 again (gift of another village to Brāhmaṇas); 1609 (gift of a village to Brāhmaṇas at the request of Mudduvīrappa Nāyaka); again in 1634 (E.I., iii, pp.236--58); 1663 (Telugu; gift of a village to a Brahman of Śrīśailam); 1665 (Tamil and Grantha; grant of lands from Nāyaka rulers to Brahmans a neighbouring village, SCP no.186); 1667 (Telugu and Grantha, gift of village lands to a Brahman) \\p.358--9 \#174 1671 - Kanarese; Ś.S. 1593, Virṃdhikrit; Viṇṇappaḷḷi (G\=opicheṭṭipālaiyam; Coimbatore); D\=evarāja Uḍaiyār divided the village into sixty-four share and granted it to sixty-four Brahmans. (I.M.P., i, p.552);},
