@Book{pras-hist,
  author           = {Beni Prasadi},
  citationkey      = {pras-hist},
  date             = {1922},
  location         = {London, etc.},
  publisher        = {Oxford University Press},
  title            = {History of Jahangir},
  series           = {Allahabad University studies in history},
  volume           = {1},
  annotation       = {Excellent annoted bibliography in appendix C.\\ p.472--3: See \emph{Letters Received by the East India Company from its Servants in the East}, 6 vols. Letters from 1602--1617; indexed and includes info on epidemics. Ditto, William Foster (ed), \emph{The English Factories in India}, 3 vols, 1618--21, 1622--23, 1624--29.\\ p.295: Contemporary chronicler Motamad Khan notes \begin{quote} When it was about to break out, a mouse would rush out of its hole as if mad, and striking itself against the dorr and the walls fo the house, would expire. If, immediately after this signal, the occupants left the house and went away to the jungle, their lives were saved; if otherwise, the inhabitants of the whole village would be swept away by the hand of death. \end{quote} 10 or 20 persons per house died in Lahore. Also in Kashmir. It lasted 8 years, 16176--1624.\\ p.290 describes pinjarapoles or veterinary hospitals in Cambay. Cites Pietro della Valle I.67--8 on these.\\ p.463: \begin{quote} The library of His Highness the Maharaja of Chhatarpur C.I. contains a Hindi MS copied from an older MS many years ago, entitled `Bundelkon ki Bansharali' or a genealogy of the Bundelas, divided into two sections. The first section is devoted to what must be called the rudiments of political philosophy, while the second traces the genealogy and marriage-relationships of the various ruling families of the Bundela race.\end{quote}\\ p.293 describes an epidemic of something like influenza that occurred in Ahmedabad in May 1618 (similar to other there in 1590, and in Calcutta 1779 \& 1824, and everywhere in 1918). Detailed account of symptoms in Edward Terry, \emph{Voyage to East India}, 226--7.\\ p.295: Bubonic plague in the Doab 1616--1624. Perhaps introduced from Central Asia in the winter of 1616. Described by Jehangir himself: \begin{quote} under the armpits or in the groin or below the throat buboes formed and they died \end{quote} (Jahangir (Rogers and Beveridge), II.65)},
  modificationdate = {2018-09-17T00:00:00},
  owner            = {dom},
  source           = {CUL: North Front, Floor 4 Classmark: RE.108.12},
}

