@TechReport{atki-repo,
  author           = {J. Atkinson},
  citationkey      = {atki-repo},
  date             = {1846},
  institution      = {Government of Bengal},
  title            = {Report of the [{Atkinson}] Committee Appointed by Government to Observe and Report upon Surgical Operations by {Dr. J. Esdaile} upon Patients under the Influence of Alleged Mesmeric Agency},
  location         = {Calcutta},
  annotation       = {members of the committee: J. Atkinson, Inspector General of Hospitals, Chair of the committee E. M. Gordon J. Jackson, Surgeon, Native Hospital D. Stweart, Presidency Surgeon W. B. O'Shaughnessy, MD, Secretary to the committee James Hume A. Rogers p.2 \P.8--9: ``8. The Mesmerizers employed by Dr. Esdaile were young men, Hindoos and Mahomedans, from 14 to 30 years of age, most of them compounders and dressers from the Hooghly Hospital. 9. To each patient a separate Mesmerizer was assigned. The room in which they operated was darkened, but from time to time the Committee were enabled to witness, through small apertures made in the door panels, the manner in which the processes were carried on. The patient lay on his back, the body naked from the waist upwards, and the thighs and legs bare; the Mesmerizer seated behind him at the head of the bed, leaning over him, the faces of both nearly in contact, the right hand being generally placed on the pit of the stomach, and passes made with one or both hands along the face, chiefly over the eyes. The Mesmerizer breathed frequently and gently over the patient's lips, eyes and nostrils. Profound silence was observed. These processes were continued for about two hours each day in ten cases, for eight hours in one cased in one day, and for six hours in another case, without interruption. \ldots 10. In seven cases [out of 10], in a period varying from one to seven sittings, deep sleep followed the performance of the processes above described. 11. This sleep in its most perfect state differed from ordinary natural sleep, as follows. The individual could not be aroused by loud noises; the pupils were insensible to light; and great, and in some cases apparently perfect, insensibility to pain was witnessed on burning, pinching, and cutting the skin and other sensitive organs. 12. This sleep in general character differed from that which would be produced by narcotic drugs, in the quickness with which , in eight cases out of ten, the patient was awoke, after certain transverse passes and fanning by the Mesmerizer, and blowing upon the face and on the eyes; in the natural condition of the pupils of the eyes and the conjunctive in all the cases after awaking; in the the absence of stertorous breathing and of subsequent delirium or hallucination, and of many other symptoms familiar to medical observers, and which are produced by Alcoholic Liquors, Opium, Hemp, and other Narcotic Drugs. \ldots 13. In seven cases Surgical operations were performed in the state of sleep above described. \ldots 31. \ldots In conclusion, the Committee are unanimously of opinion that great credit is due to Dr. Estaile for the zeal, ability, and boldness with which he has taken up and pursued this enquiry. His sphere, however, has been hitherto limited, but the Committee hope that his further investigations may be extended to Medical as well as Surgical cases, to European as well as Native patients, and to the elucidation of the several questions which have been adverted to in the course of this report. E. M. Gordon, Evelyn M. Gordon. D. Stewart, Presidency Surgeon James Hume J. Jackson, Surgeon to the Native Hospital A. Rogers W. B. O'Shaughnessy, MD, Secretary to the committee Calcutta, 7th October, 1846. p. 6: a Minute by Mr Hume aimed at giving a more favourable version of Mesmerism than that given by O'Shaughnessy in the main report. Ends, pp.28--9, with a letter from Fred. Jas. Halliday, Sec. to the Govt. of Bengal, to Atkinson and O'Shaughnessy, 4 Nov 1896. Esdaile is placed in charge of a small experimental hospital in Calcutta, in order that he {5}, as recommended by the Committee, extend his investigations to the applicability of this alleged agency to all descriptions of cases, Medical as well as Surgical, and all classes of patients, European as well as Native.},
  keywords         = {mesmerism hypnosis hypnotism Esdaile Calcutta},
  modificationdate = {2018-04-09T00:00:00},
  owner            = {dom},
  source           = {BL IOR V/26/850/11},
}

